{
  "id": "personalpronomen",
  "name": "Subject pronouns: je, tu, il, elle, on, nous, vous",
  "level": "A1",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "variety": "fr-FR",
  "frCA": { "note": "In Québec people say tu more, often right away, also with new colleagues. Vous stays for strangers, older people and offices." },
  "subtitle": "Je, tu, il, elle, on, nous, vous, ils, elles. French always needs the pronoun before the verb: Je parle français. Use tu for a friend and vous for a stranger or several people; on means “we” in speech.",
  "briefMarks": ["je", "tu", "il", "elle", "on", "nous", "vous", "moi"],
  "source": "icanspeak French original text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-11-08)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      { "text": "pronoun", "variant": "prep" },
      { "text": "verb", "variant": "verb" },
      { "text": "rest" }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-prep\">Je</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">suis</span> Hugo. · <span class=\"vp-prep\">J'</span><span class=\"vp-verb\">habite</span> à Lyon. · <span class=\"vp-prep\">Vous</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">travaillez</span> ici<em> ?</em> · <span class=\"vp-prep\">On</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">mange</span> ensemble. · <span class=\"vp-prep\">Elles</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">sont</span> de Nantes.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>tu</strong> is for one person you know well: friends, family, children, classmates, and often colleagues. <em>Tu habites où ?</em>",
        "tag": "tu"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>vous</strong> is polite for <em>one</em> person: a stranger, an older person, an official, a doctor. <em>Vous habitez ici ?</em> It is also the plural: <em>vous</em> for two or more people, even friends. If you are not sure, start with <em>vous</em>.",
        "tag": "vous"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>il, elle, ils, elles</strong> also mean “it” and “they” for things: <em>Le bus arrive. Il est en retard.</em> <em>Ils</em> is for men or a mixed group. <em>Elles</em> is only for women.",
        "tag": "il, elle, ils, elles"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>on</strong> means “we” in everyday speech. The verb is the same as for <em>il</em>: <em>On habite à Lille.</em> <em>Nous</em> is the same meaning, a bit more formal: <em>Nous habitons à Lille.</em>",
        "tag": "on = we"
      },
      {
        "html": "French always says the pronoun. The ending is often silent: <em>je parle, tu parles, il parle, ils parlent</em> sound the same. So never drop it: <em>Je travaille.</em> Not: <em>Travaille.</em>",
        "tag": "Always say it"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Stress pronouns</strong> <em>moi, toi, lui, elle, nous, vous, eux, elles</em> add emphasis or follow a preposition: <em>Moi, je travaille. Et toi ?</em> <em>avec moi, chez lui, pour eux</em>. They never replace <em>je</em>, <em>tu</em>… before the verb.",
        "tag": "moi, toi, lui"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>Parle français</em> → ✓ <em>Je parle français</em>. ✗ <em>Je habite</em> → ✓ <em>J'habite</em>. ✗ <em>Moi suis infirmière</em> → ✓ <em>Moi, je suis infirmière</em>. Next: <a href=\"/present-regular\">the present of -er verbs</a> and <a href=\"/stem-changing-verbs\">être and avoir</a>.",
    "reference": {
